Recognize the CSI-tilde form of F1-F4 when it carries modifier or event
parameters, so F3 is not dropped.

The herdr client pushes the kitty keyboard protocol onto the host terminal with
REPORT_EVENT_TYPES set (src/client/mod.rs push_keyboard_enhancement_flags,
src/input/model.rs ime_compatible_keyboard_enhancement_flags -> flags 7). A
terminal in that mode appends a modifier/event parameter to every functional
key, and F3 has no letter form in the protocol: kitty assigns it number 13 with
the `~` final byte precisely because `CSI 1;m R` collides with the cursor
position report. foot emits `ESC [ 13 ; 1 : 1 ~` for a plain F3 press.

parse_terminal_key_sequence's exact-match table covers the bare `ESC [ 13 ~`,
but the parameterized form falls through to
parse_xterm_modified_special_sequence, whose `~` table starts at 15. F3 (and
the tilde form of F1, F2 and F4) therefore returns None and raw_input drops the
sequence as unsupported: the key is swallowed, no keybinding fires and nothing
reaches the focused pane. F1, F2 and F4 escape this in practice because the
protocol keeps their letter finals (P, Q, S), which the branch above already
handles, and F5 upwards are already listed here.

The three assertions that pinned the old behaviour are inverted rather than
deleted, so the case stays covered.

--- a/src/input/parse.rs
+++ b/src/input/parse.rs
@@ -216,6 +216,10 @@
         "3" => KeyCode::Delete,
         "5" => KeyCode::PageUp,
         "6" => KeyCode::PageDown,
+        "11" => KeyCode::F(1),
+        "12" => KeyCode::F(2),
+        "13" => KeyCode::F(3),
+        "14" => KeyCode::F(4),
         "15" => KeyCode::F(5),
         "17" => KeyCode::F(6),
         "18" => KeyCode::F(7),
@@ -575,9 +579,36 @@
             crossterm::event::KeyEventKind::Press,
             None,
         );
-        assert_eq!(parse_terminal_key_sequence("\x1b[11;2~"), None);
-        assert_eq!(parse_terminal_key_sequence("\x1b[14;1~"), None);
-        assert_eq!(parse_terminal_key_sequence("\x1b[14;3~"), None);
+        assert_terminal_key_eq(
+            parse_terminal_key_sequence("\x1b[11;2~").expect("shift+f1 should parse"),
+            KeyCode::F(1),
+            KeyModifiers::SHIFT,
+            crossterm::event::KeyEventKind::Press,
+            None,
+        );
+        assert_terminal_key_eq(
+            parse_terminal_key_sequence("\x1b[14;1~").expect("f4 should parse"),
+            KeyCode::F(4),
+            KeyModifiers::empty(),
+            crossterm::event::KeyEventKind::Press,
+            None,
+        );
+        // The kitty keyboard protocol has no letter form for F3, so a host with
+        // REPORT_EVENT_TYPES enabled sends plain F3 as this sequence.
+        assert_terminal_key_eq(
+            parse_terminal_key_sequence("\x1b[13;1:1~").expect("f3 should parse"),
+            KeyCode::F(3),
+            KeyModifiers::empty(),
+            crossterm::event::KeyEventKind::Press,
+            None,
+        );
+        assert_terminal_key_eq(
+            parse_terminal_key_sequence("\x1b[13;2~").expect("shift+f3 should parse"),
+            KeyCode::F(3),
+            KeyModifiers::SHIFT,
+            crossterm::event::KeyEventKind::Press,
+            None,
+        );
     }

     #[test]
